  "query_preview": "/Through Tuesday/...\nIssued at 133 PM MDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nAnother warm day in the books for October as current surface\nobservations show widespread temperatures in the low-to-mid 80s.\nWith the increased cloud cover today, we should stay slightly\ncooler compared to yesterday. However, near-record temperatures\nare still expected this afternoon for portions of the plains\n(record temperature at DIA is 87, with forecast high temperature\nof 86 today). Isolated light showers and possibly a few rumbles of\nthunder still look on track this afternoon and evening for the\nmountains and high valleys, with current radar imagery already\nshowing pop-up convection near South Park. Precipitation amounts\nwill be light with anything that does develop. While unlikely (~10%\nchance or less) CAMs do indicate a few virga showers spilling \nonto the I-25 Corridor later this evening, with gusty outflow \nwinds of 25-35 mph possible. \n\nWarm and mostly dry conditions look to continue through the end of\nthe week and into the weekend, as an upper level ridge resides \nover the southwestern U.S. Highs should be about 10-15 dg F above \nnormal. For Wednesday and Thursday, 700-mb temperatures will \n\"cool\" slightly, which will help keep temperatures in the low 80s \nacross the plains. Like today, another round of isolated shower \nactivity is possible, but should mostly be confined to the higher \nterrain. With flow aloft turning more southwesterly, precipitation\nchances decrease Friday and Saturday. \n\nA pattern change is becoming more likely by Sunday/Monday, as a\nsystem from the Pacific northwest dips south along California\nearly next week before eventually ejecting east by mid-to-late\nnext week. However, there is still a lot of model variability in\nterms of evolution of the system and moisture availability. \nCooler temperatures should arrive by Sunday or Monday for our \nforecast area behind a cold front. However, it is uncertain\nexactly when and how cool we will get, as ensemble spread for max\nT ranges from high 50s to low 80s both days. Regardless, we can\nexpect cooler conditions. In terms of precipitation, guidance does\ntry to pull in moisture from the remnants of Hurricane Rachel. If\nthe moisture can travel up to our region, shower activity will\nreturn to the mountains. Unfortunately for the plains, it doesn't\nlook like any meaningful precipitation could occur until mid-to- \nlate next week.",
